# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# TensorBoard / training logs
**/logs/
**/events.out.tfevents*

# Datasets cached at runtime (labs re-download them on demand)
**/data_cache/
*.h5
*.keras

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Office owner files, written while a deck is open in PowerPoint and deleted
# when it closes. Committing one ships a 165-byte stub next to a real deck.
~$*

# NOTE: generated .pptx and .pdf are deliberately NOT ignored.
# This repository is also the distribution channel: students must receive
# the built slides and handouts with a plain `git pull`, without needing
# pandoc installed. See tools/build.py.

# Python virtual environments (WSL/local toolchain)
.venv/
venv/

# Worked solutions to the exercises: generated for the instructor,
# never distributed to students.
*_solution.ipynb
*_solution.md
Solutions/
# ...and any script that writes one. The notebook is covered above, but the
# generator carries the same answers and the same marking notes, and its name
# ends in .py rather than .ipynb. A pattern rather than the one literal name
# lesson 9 happened to use: the skill asks only for a leading underscore, so
# _build_solution.py and make_solution.py are both plausible, and neither was
# covered.
*_solution.py

# The instructor's own study and teaching notes: what to say with the projector
# off, the derivations to reproduce at the board, the questions students will
# ask. Private working material, not a course artefact — students receive the
# handouts, which carry the same mathematics written for them.
Instructor/
